\caption(Actual compensation feeds a lost contribution into the next update.)
\alttext(Four producing states display the actual rounded sum and correction, ending at two after the large values cancel.)

> Derive the actual update, without claiming exact lost bits

The recurrence is

$$
y=x-c,\quad t=s+y,\quad c=(t-s)-y,\quad s=t.
$$
\alttext(Subtract the prior correction from the new value, add to the running sum, estimate the rounded loss, then commit the new sum.)

Every displayed arithmetic operation rounds in double. The correction estimates
a local rounding effect; it is not an exact residual expansion. Unsafe fast-math
reassociation can turn the intended correction into zero, so the operation order
must be preserved. A finite-sum relative-error statement also depends on
cancellation conditioning, often expressed by sum of absolute inputs divided by
absolute exact sum. Near a zero exact total that ratio can be enormous or
undefined. The old unconditional n-independent relative-accuracy claim was
therefore too strong.

> Retain one million ones between cancelling large values

The original stress test is 1e16, one million actual additions of one, then
−1e16. Its represented inputs are integers and their exact real sum is one
million. The native stream performs every original update with the same
recurrence; streaming changes storage, not the sequence or algorithm. A naive
loop provides the baseline.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kahan_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/kahan.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::KahanAccumulator a;
    (void) a.add(1e16);
    a.add_repeated(1, 1000000);
    (void) a.add(-1e16);
    double naive = 1e16;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 1000000; ++i)
        naive += 1;
    naive -= 1e16;
    std::print("| method | actual sum | exact integer target | absolute error |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: " "|\n| naive | {:.17g} | 1000000 | {:.17g} |\n| Kahan | {:.17g} | 1000000 | {:.17g} |\n",
               naive, std::fabs(naive - 1000000), a.sum(),
               std::fabs(a.sum() - 1000000));
    std::print("\nThe native stream completed {} actual updates.\n", a.count());
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points({0, 1}, {naive, a.sum()}, "", {37, 99, 235}, 7).title(
        "Original million-one cancellation experiment").x_label(
        "method code: 0 naive, 1 Kahan").y_label("actual final sum").y_limits(
        -50000, 1200000).save_svg("build/kahan_original.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| method | actual sum | exact integer target | absolute error |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| naive | 0 | 1000000 | 1000000 |
| Kahan | 1000000 | 1000000 | 0 |

The native stream completed 1000002 actual updates.
\image(figures/kahan_original.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual results of the full original million-one stress test.)
\alttext(The two plotted method outputs are compared after the same cancelling large endpoints and one million intervening additions.)

> Compensation can still lose a term

For [1e16,1,−1e16], the exact represented-input sum is one, yet Kahan can return
zero: adjusting the final huge term rounds away the pending unit. Permuting to
[1e16,−1e16,1] returns one. This is a limitation of the method, not evidence
that its correction has been omitted. Empty input returns zero. Nonfinite values
propagate by ordinary IEEE arithmetic; after an infinity, the compensation can
become NaN.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kahan_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/kahan.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::print("| control | actual Kahan sum | exact finite target |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    const std::vector<std::pair<const char *, std::vector<double>> > cases = {
        {"empty", {}}, {"lost singleton", {1e16, 1, -1e16}},
        {"permuted", {1e16, -1e16, 1}},
        {"two recovered ones", {1e16, 1, 1, -1e16}}
    };
    const double targets[] = {0, 1, 1, 2};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < cases.size(); ++i) {
        const auto value = dm::algorithms::kahan_sum(cases[i].second);
        std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {} |\n", cases[i].first, value, targets[i]);
        x.push_back(i);
        y.push_back(value);
    }
    bool capped = false;
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::kahan_trace({1, 2, 3}, 2);
    } catch (const std::length_error &) {
        capped = true;
    }
    if (!capped)
        throw std::runtime_error("complete quota");
    std::print("\nA two-record ceiling rejects the complete three-value trace.\n");
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "actual", {37, 99, 235}, 6).points(x, {0, 1, 1, 2}, "exact",
                                                      {220, 38, 38}, 3).title(
        "Cancellation and order controls").x_label("control row ordinal").y_label("final sum").y_limits(-.2, 2.8).save_svg("build/kahan_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| control | actual Kahan sum | exact finite target |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| empty | 0 | 0 |
| lost singleton | 0 | 1 |
| permuted | 1 | 1 |
| two recovered ones | 2 | 2 |

A two-record ceiling rejects the complete three-value trace.
\image(figures/kahan_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A finite cancellation control remains inaccurate and order-dependent.)
\alttext(The actual lost-singleton result zero differs from its exact target one, while the permuted and two-one controls attain their targets.)

> Retain every repeated-0.1 size through one hundred million

The original sizes are 1,000 through 100,000,000 by factors of ten. Each size
performs all updates without a repeated-vector allocation. The input value is
binary64 0.1, whose exact represented value differs from decimal one tenth. To
measure summation error against the exact product of that represented input and
integer n, the program uses a single fused multiply-add of (−n,value,sum). For
these finite controls it gives the correctly rounded error against the exact
product, rather than subtracting the already rounded double product n*value.
Independent exact rational verification passed for all six repeat counts.
Logarithms use positive measured errors; no artificial floor is substituted for
zero.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kahan_repeats.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/kahan.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, naive_error, kahan_error;
    std::print("| actual repeats | naive sum | Kahan sum | naive absolute summation error | Kahan absolute " "summation error |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n = 1000; n <= 100000000; n *= 10) {
        dm::algorithms::KahanAccumulator a;
        a.add_repeated(.1, n);
        double naive = 0;
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
            naive += .1;
        const double en =
            std::fabs(std::fma(-double(n), .1, naive)), ek =
            std::fabs(std::fma(-double(n), .1, a.sum()));
        if (!(en > 0 && ek > 0))
            throw std::runtime_error("positive error for logarithmic diagnostic");
        std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} | {:.12e} | {:.12e} |\n", n, naive,
                   a.sum(), en, ek);
        x.push_back(std::log10(n));
        naive_error.push_back(std::log10(en));
        kahan_error.push_back(std::log10(ek));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, naive_error, "naive", {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(x, naive_error, "",
                                                             {220, 38, 38}, 4).line(x, kahan_error, "Kahan", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, kahan_error, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4).title("Full streamed repeated-value experiments").x_label("log10(actual repeat count)").y_label("log10(absolute summation error)").y_limits(-16, 1).save_svg("build/kahan_repeats.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual repeats | naive sum | Kahan sum | naive absolute summation error | Kahan absolute summation error |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1000 | 99.999999999998593 | 100 | 1.412425731928e-12 | 5.551115123126e-15 |
| 10000 | 1000.0000000001588 | 1000 | 1.587650011459e-10 | 5.551115123126e-14 |
| 100000 | 10000.000000018848 | 10000 | 1.884781308803e-08 | 5.551115123126e-13 |
| 1000000 | 100000.00000133288 | 100000 | 1.332877124227e-06 | 5.551115123126e-12 |
| 10000000 | 999999.99983897537 | 1000000 | 1.610246809243e-04 | 5.551115123126e-11 |
| 100000000 | 9999999.9811294507 | 10000000 | 1.887054983158e-02 | 5.551115123126e-10 |
\image(figures/kahan_repeats.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual summation errors against exact represented-input products.)
\alttext(Both methods execute every original repeat count; the log-scale errors exclude decimal input-representation error and retain measured finite values.)

The scalar API and stream use linear arithmetic and constant storage with no
shared state or input mutation. Stream repeat admission checks count overflow
before all updates. Full tracing additionally owns one actual arithmetic record
per input, admits the complete count before allocation, and defaults to a
million records with zero disabling the optional ceiling. These limits count
records, excluding vector capacity, byte counts and peak RAM. Failure exposes no
partial trace. No external dataset is needed to study these controlled
arithmetic effects.

Independent rounded-operation checks verified 1,000 arrays and 30,000 actual
update records. Python Fraction references verified all six full repeat
experiments through 100,000,000 updates against the exact represented-input
product. Another 1,000 arrays matched the frozen original recurrence exactly;
native tests cover nonfinite propagation, complete history ceilings, streaming
and count-overflow admission. The shared ten-report build passed 131 native
tests, 38 directly instrumented address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer tests, 89
compiled public examples, documentation coverage for all 388 scoped definitions
and ten representative clangd hovers. All four complete C++ report programs
executed successfully and their native figures were visually reviewed. Batch
receipts are retained under `build/mepml-conversion/batch-174-183/`.

> Reference

William Kahan (1965), “Pracniques: Further Remarks on Reducing Truncation
Errors”, Communications of the ACM8(1),40,
[doi:10.1145/363707.363723|https://doi.org/10.1145/363707.363723]. The present
report retains the specific sequential compensation recurrence while separating
its practical improvements from exactness and conditioning claims.
